.TH EXPOSE 1 "2026-10-10" "expose v0.4.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
expose \- secure tunneling and reverse proxy tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expose
[\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGUMENTS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBexpose\fR is an open-source, single-binary reverse-proxy and secure
tunneling tool that connects local HTTP and TCP servers to the public
internet with zero account signup required.
.PP
Think of \fBexpose\fR like a digital courier: public visitors send
requests to a unique public URL, \fBexpose\fR transfers the packets through
an encrypted tunnel directly into your local workstation's server port,
and streams the responses back to the visitor in real time.
.PP
Supported tunnel backends:
.TP
\fBlocaltunnel\fR
Free community-driven tunneling infrastructure (default provider).
.TP
\fBcloudflare\fR
High-performance Quick Tunnels powered by the Cloudflare global network
(requires \fBcloudflared\fR installed).
.TP
\fBselfhosted\fR
Private tunneling server running on your own VPS with custom domain routing.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBinit\fR
Initialize a new \fB.expose.yml\fR configuration file in the current
working directory.
.TP
\fBtunnel\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Start an encrypted tunnel session to expose a local port to the internet.
.TP
\fBserver\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Start a self-hosted tunnel control plane and HTTP reverse-proxy server on a VPS.
.TP
\fBconfig\fR \fISUBCOMMAND\fR
Inspect and manage configuration values stored in \fB.expose.yml\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBlist\fR
List all configuration key-value pairs.
.TP
\fBget\fR \fIKEY\fR
Retrieve the value of a specific configuration key
(e.g. \fBport\fR, \fBproject\fR).
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global Options
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display help information for \fBexpose\fR or any subcommand.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display version, git commit hash, and build date.
.SS Tunnel Options (\fBtunnel\fR)
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR
Local port to expose (e.g. 3000, 8080). If omitted, falls back to the
port specified in \fB.expose.yml\fR.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-provider\fR \fINAME\fR
Tunnel provider to use: \fBlocaltunnel\fR, \fBcloudflare\fR, or
\fBselfhosted\fR (default: \fBlocaltunnel\fR).
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-server\fR \fIHOST:PORT\fR
Self-hosted server address (e.g. \fItunnel.mysite.com:7890\fR). Providing
this flag automatically selects the \fBselfhosted\fR provider.
.SS Server Options (\fBserver\fR)
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-domain\fR \fIDOMAIN\fR
Base domain name for public tunnel URLs (default: \fIlocalhost\fR).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-control-port\fR \fIPORT\fR
TCP port for tunnel client control and data connections (default: \fI7890\fR).
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-public-port\fR \fIPORT\fR
TCP port for incoming public visitor HTTP traffic (default: \fI8080\fR).
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fBexpose\fR looks for a configuration file named \fB.expose.yml\fR in the
current working directory.
.PP
A template file can be generated by running \fBexpose init\fR.
.PP
Supported configuration fields:
.TP
\fBproject\fR
Project name identifier (defaults to the current directory name).
.TP
\fBport\fR
Default local port to forward (default: \fI3000\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB.expose.yml\fR
Project configuration file in the current working directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBPATH\fR
The system executable path used to discover the \fBcloudflared\fR binary
when using the Cloudflare provider.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success.
.TP
\fB1\fR
General error (e.g. missing configuration, invalid port range, connection
timeout, or server error).
.SH EXAMPLES
Expose the default port (from \fB.expose.yml\fR) via LocalTunnel:
.RS
.B expose tunnel
.RE
.PP
Expose a specific port (e.g. 3000) via LocalTunnel:
.RS
.B expose tunnel \-p 3000
.RE
.PP
Expose port 8080 using Cloudflare Quick Tunnels:
.RS
.B expose tunnel \-p 8080 \-P cloudflare
.RE
.PP
Expose port 3000 using a custom self-hosted server:
.RS
.B expose tunnel \-p 3000 \-s tunnel.mysite.com:7890
.RE
.PP
Run a self-hosted tunnel server on your VPS:
.RS
.B expose server \-\-domain=tunnel.mysite.com \-\-control-port=7890 \-\-public-port=8080
.RE
.PP
List all saved configuration settings:
.RS
.B expose config list
.RE
.PP
Get the current configured port:
.RS
.B expose config get port
.RE
.SH INSTALLATION
To install the man page into the system manual path:
.RS
.B sudo install \-d /usr/local/share/man/man1
.br
.B sudo install \-m 0644 man/expose.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/expose.1
.RE
.PP
On Linux distributions with \fBmandb\fR:
.RS
.B sudo mandb
.RE
.PP
To view the man page locally without installing:
.RS
.B man ./man/expose.1
.RE
